---
import ArticlePage from '../../components/ArticlePage.astro';
import { sitePath } from '../../utils/paths';
import { HANDICAP_PUBLISHED, USGA_EXPECTED_SCORE_URL, USGA_HANDICAP_INDEX_URL } from '../../data/handicapSources';
import { HANDICAP_CALLS, HANDICAP_TARGET } from '../../data/storeCalls';


const FEW_ROUNDS: readonly (readonly [string, string])[] = [
  ['3', 'Lowest 1, minus 2.0'],
  ['4', 'Lowest 1, minus 1.0'],
  ['5', 'Lowest 1'],
  ['6', 'Average of lowest 2, minus 1.0'],
  ['7 to 8', 'Average of lowest 2'],
  ['9 to 11', 'Average of lowest 3'],
  ['12 to 14', 'Average of lowest 4'],
  ['15 to 16', 'Average of lowest 5'],
  ['17 to 18', 'Average of lowest 6'],
  ['19', 'Average of lowest 7'],
  ['20', 'Average of lowest 8'],
];
---
<ArticlePage
  pageTitle="How is a golf handicap calculated? · SundayStroke"
  description="How a golf handicap is worked out under the World Handicap System: the score differential, course rating and slope, the best 8 of your last 20 rounds, and what to do with fewer rounds."
  eyebrow="Guide · Golf handicap"
  heading="How a golf handicap is calculated"
  published={HANDICAP_PUBLISHED}
  banner={HANDICAP_TARGET}
  calls={HANDICAP_CALLS}
>
  <p>
    A handicap says how many strokes over a course's difficulty you usually play on a good day. Under the World Handicap System it
    comes from three things for each round: your score, the course rating and the slope. Here is how they fit together.
  </p>

  <h2>1. The score differential</h2>
  <p>Every 18-hole round becomes one number, the score differential:</p>
  <p><strong>Score differential = (113 ÷ slope) × (adjusted gross score − course rating)</strong></p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Course rating</strong> is what a scratch golfer is expected to shoot from your tees, for example 72.0.</li>
    <li><strong>Slope</strong> says how much harder the course plays for everyone else. 113 is a course of standard difficulty, so on a slope of 113 the differential is simply your score minus the course rating.</li>
    <li><strong>Adjusted gross score</strong> is your score with each hole capped at net double bogey, so one disaster hole doesn't count in full.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Example: 90 on a course rated 72.0 with a slope of 113 gives a differential of 18.0. The same 90 on a slope of 130 would count for less, because the course is harder.</p>

  <h2>2. The best 8 of your last 20</h2>
  <p>
    With 20 rounds, your handicap is the average of your best 8 score differentials from your last 20 rounds. The other 12 are left out,
    so a handicap shows what you can do, not your average day
    (<a href={USGA_HANDICAP_INDEX_URL}>USGA: the Handicap Index calculation</a>).
  </p>

  <h3>Fewer than 20 rounds</h3>
  <p>You need at least 3 rounds. Until you have 20, fewer differentials count, and with very few rounds a small adjustment is taken off:</p>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Rounds</th><th>What counts</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      {FEW_ROUNDS.map(([rounds, rule]) => <tr><td>{rounds}</td><td>{rule}</td></tr>)}
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>The result is rounded to one decimal and can't go above 54.0. A result below zero is a plus handicap, written like +1.5.</p>

  <h2>Nine-hole rounds</h2>
  <p>
    Official systems turn a nine-hole score into an 18-hole one by adding an expected score for the other nine holes
    (<a href={USGA_EXPECTED_SCORE_URL}>USGA: expected score explained</a>). Our calculator doesn't do that, so it covers 18-hole rounds only.
    Golf GPS counts a nine-hole round too, more simply: it doubles that round's differential against half the course rating.
  </p>

  <h2>How Golf GPS's Estimated Handicap relates to this</h2>
  <p>
    Golf GPS Pro uses the same rules for the rounds you play with it: the score differential above, the few-rounds table and the best 8
    of your last 20. One difference: the app caps every hole at par + 5 rather than net double bogey. That's why the app calls it an Estimated Handicap:
    it is not an official Handicap Index. It leaves out things only your club or federation does, such as adjusting for the day's
    playing conditions and the caps on how fast an official index can rise. For an official index, join a golf club or your national federation.
  </p>

  <h2>Try it</h2>
  <p>Enter your own rounds in the <a href={sitePath('/golf-handicap/calculator/')}>handicap calculator</a>.</p>
</ArticlePage>
